1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,120
A hot bath was medicine. A hot bath was
also, according to the very same doctors, a

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:06,920
way to let death into your body.

3
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:09,350
It's sitting right there
in the historical record.

4
00:00:09,370 --> 00:00:13,760
Physicians in medieval Europe genuinely
believed both things at once, sometimes in

5
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:17,560
the same document. And once you understand
why, you'll understand something most

6
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:21,590
people get completely wrong about how
the Middle Ages actually thought.

7
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:23,780
You've probably heard the
other version of this story.

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:29,400
Medieval people didn't bathe. Filthy
streets, filthy bodies, centuries of grime,

9
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:34,070
until soap and running water finally rescued
Europe from the Dark Ages of hygiene.

10
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:35,490
It's a satisfying story.

11
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,010
It's also mostly wrong.

12
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:40,820
Medieval Europeans bathed. They
built entire institutions around it.

13
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:45,060
And then, during the worst disease outbreaks
in European history, doctors began

14
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:49,520
warning that the very thing keeping people
clean could also make them vulnerable to

15
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:53,340
disease. Not because they were simply
ignorant, but because they understood the

16
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:56,920
human body through a framework
that made that conclusion logical.

17
00:00:56,940 --> 00:01:00,380
So logical that a king commissioned
an official report about it.

18
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:05,060
To understand how a bath became a weapon,
start with what a bath actually was.

19
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:08,020
Picture a medieval European
city in the 1300s.

20
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:09,070
You need to wash.

21
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:13,170
If you're wealthy, maybe you have a tub
at home, heated over a fire by servants.

22
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:15,830
But if you're like most people,
you go to the bathhouse.

23
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:19,740
A medieval bathhouse wasn't some
grim, joyless tub in a back room.

24
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:23,470
It was closer to a public square
that happened to have water in it.

25
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:25,600
People sat together in shared tubs.

26
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:28,110
There was food, drink, and conversation.

27
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:32,740
Bathhouses were places to get groomed,
socialize, and sometimes receive medical

28
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:36,900
treatment. In some places, they were
also associated with prostitution.

29
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:41,040
Cities regulated them. In Paris, there
were enough public baths that some streets

30
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:45,660
were named after them. Underneath all of
it was soap — real soap, made from animal

31
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:50,140
fat and wood ash, sometimes scented with
herbs, and traded across Europe since at

32
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:51,510
least the ninth century.

33
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:56,070
Medieval Europeans were washing themselves,
in public, as a normal part of life.

34
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:57,700
So where does the fear come from?

35
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:01,580
You have to stop thinking about bathing as
hygiene, and start thinking about it as

36
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:02,460
medicine.

37
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,340
Medieval medicine didn't have germs.

38
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:05,360
It had humors.

39
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:09,350
Four of them: blood, phlegm,
yellow bile, and black bile.

40
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:11,790
Health wasn't the absence
of invisible pathogens.

41
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:16,680
Health was balance — a body correctly
proportioned between hot and cold, wet and

42
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:20,920
dry. And heat and moisture weren't
just sensations to a medieval doctor.

43
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:24,710
They were forces that physically
changed the body's internal state.

44
00:02:24,730 --> 00:02:28,250
A hot bath, under this
framework, wasn't just relaxing.

45
00:02:28,270 --> 00:02:33,160
It was a medical tool. It could warm a
cold constitution, loosen what was stiff,

46
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:36,930
open the body, and encourage it to
sweat out what needed releasing.

47
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:41,380
Physicians prescribed baths for conditions
including fevers, joint pain, and

48
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:45,320
headaches, adjusting the temperature and
sometimes adding herbs depending on the

49
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:49,600
effect they wanted. This wasn't folk
superstition sitting outside real medicine.

50
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:54,330
This was the medicine. And here's the part
that sounds strangest to a modern ear.

51
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:58,440
Medieval physicians didn't think of
skin as a sealed barrier the way we do.

52
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:02,480
They thought of it as porous, full of tiny
openings that allowed things to move in

53
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:03,500
both directions.

54
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:05,360
Heat opened those openings wider.

55
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:09,120
That's how a bath worked. It didn't just
clean the surface — it changed what could

56
00:03:09,140 --> 00:03:11,200
pass through the body. Hold onto that.

57
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:16,680
Heat opens the body. Because in 1348, that
medical idea was about to collide with

58
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:19,470
something Europe had
never seen on that scale.

59
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:24,320
The Black Death moved through European
cities with terrifying speed, killing a huge

60
00:03:24,340 --> 00:03:25,650
share of the population.

61
00:03:25,860 --> 00:03:30,420
Modern estimates vary widely, but the
scale of the catastrophe was unmistakable.

62
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:33,400
And physicians were trying to
explain it with the tools they had.

63
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:37,720
One explanation that became influential
was miasma: the idea that corrupted or

64
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:39,420
poisoned air could carry disease.

65
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:44,540
This wasn't a stupid idea. Sickness
clustered in crowded, foul-smelling places.

66
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:48,380
Given what physicians could observe, it
made sense to suspect the air itself.

67
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:50,580
Now put the two ideas next to each other.

68
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:54,000
A hot bath opens the pores.
Corrupted air is dangerous.

69
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:56,000
What happens if you combine them?

70
00:03:56,020 --> 00:04:01,380
In October 1348, King Philip the sixth of
France commissioned the medical faculty of

71
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:05,490
the University of Paris to produce an
official report on the pestilence — what was

72
00:04:05,510 --> 00:04:08,500
causing it, and what people
should do to survive it.

73
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:12,580
That report still exists. A document
written by physicians during the outbreak

74
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:18,300
itself. Inside it, alongside advice about
diet, sleep, and emotional state, is a

75
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:20,300
section on exercise and bathing.

76
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:24,080
The faculty warned that bathing could
increase a person's vulnerability to the

77
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:24,700
plague.

78
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:29,060
Not because water itself was evil, but
because a hot bath opened the body, and an

79
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:33,460
open body, in air they believed was already
corrupted, was a body with its defenses

80
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:36,350
down. This is where the
contradiction disappears.

81
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:40,480
The same mechanism that made a bath
therapeutic — opening the body to produce a

82
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:45,280
desired effect — could, under a different
set of circumstances, make that same bath

83
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:49,340
dangerous. Nobody had to abandon
their theory of how the body worked.

84
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:51,310
They simply applied it to a new threat.

85
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:55,440
Other plague writings from the period make
the logic even more direct: warm water

86
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:56,550
opened the pores,

87
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,560
corrupted air could enter more easily
through them, so bathing was something to

88
00:05:00,580 --> 00:05:05,110
avoid. But before you file this away as
"medieval doctors were simply wrong,"

89
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:07,320
there's something the
story usually leaves out.

90
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:10,090
Miasma wasn't the only
theory in circulation.

91
00:05:10,110 --> 00:05:14,960
In Granada, a physician named Ibn al-Khatib
was watching the same plague unfold and

92
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:17,930
noticed something the air
theory didn't fully explain.

93
00:05:17,950 --> 00:05:20,950
People who had contact with
the sick were getting sick.

94
00:05:20,970 --> 00:05:25,140
Not everyone breathing the same air —
specifically, people near the infected.

95
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:29,520
He argued for something much closer to
contagion: disease passing from person to

96
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:32,150
person rather than simply
floating through the air.

97
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:36,690
Another physician, Ibn Khātima, reached
similar conclusions around the same time.

98
00:05:36,710 --> 00:05:41,940
So this wasn't a monolith. It wasn't
"medieval people believed one simple, wrong

99
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:42,700
thing."

100
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:46,260
It was a live medical debate, with
different physicians drawing different

101
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:48,340
conclusions from what they were observing.

102
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:51,760
Some of them were closer to the truth
than we usually give them credit for.

103
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:55,300
There was also another reason to avoid
the bathhouse that had nothing to do with

104
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:58,060
pores or air. Bathhouses were crowded.

105
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:02,560
During an epidemic, a room full of strangers,
some of whom might already be sick, is

106
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:07,010
a genuinely reasonable place to avoid,
even without a correct theory of germs.

107
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:08,910
Medieval physicians noticed this too.

108
00:06:08,930 --> 00:06:13,080
Crowding and the risk of contact with
infected people appeared alongside the pore

109
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:14,990
theory as reasons to stay away.

110
00:06:15,010 --> 00:06:19,460
Some of the fear was simply people noticing
that gathering in a warm, enclosed room

111
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:22,300
during a mass death event
probably wasn't a great idea.

112
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:24,690
So now you'd expect the obvious ending.

113
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:25,990
The Black Death hits.

114
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:27,550
Doctors warn against bathing.

115
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:28,830
The bathhouses close.

116
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:32,730
Europe stops bathing and stays dirty
for the next few hundred years.

117
00:06:32,750 --> 00:06:34,090
Except that's not what happened.

118
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:36,630
Bathhouses didn't disappear in 1349.

119
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:40,560
They didn't disappear in 1360.
They continued for generations.

120
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:41,810
They kept serving food.

121
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:44,270
They kept providing social
and medical functions.

122
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:47,970
People kept going. The plague changed
the calculation during outbreaks.

123
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:52,260
It didn't end the institution. Which means
the real question isn't how the Black

124
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:53,670
Death destroyed bathing.

125
00:06:53,820 --> 00:06:57,340
It's: if the plague didn't
kill the bathhouse, what did?

126
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:01,730
The answer arrives more than a century later,
and it has much less to do with pores.

127
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:05,790
At the very end of the 15th century,
a new disease appeared in Europe.

128
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:10,380
Syphilis. And unlike plague, syphilis
became strongly associated with sexual

129
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:11,260
contact.

130
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:15,590
By this point, some bathhouses already
had reputations that went beyond washing,

131
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:19,100
including associations with
prostitution and sexual activity.

132
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:23,050
Then a new disease appeared that could
be transmitted through sexual contact.

133
00:07:23,070 --> 00:07:26,290
The combination changed how some
authorities viewed the bathhouse.

134
00:07:26,310 --> 00:07:28,080
You can see it in the municipal record.

135
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:33,880
In Zurich, in 1496, as syphilis spread, the
city council issued regulations ordering

136
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:37,430
people with the disease to stay
away from inns and bathhouses.

137
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:41,460
Barbers and surgeons were restricted from
admitting infected people to the baths,

138
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:44,800
and rules were imposed concerning
potentially contaminated laundry.

139
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:47,700
This wasn't just vague cultural anxiety.

140
00:07:47,720 --> 00:07:51,900
It was a government, in writing, treating
the bathhouse as a potential point of

141
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,070
contagion for a specific disease.

142
00:07:54,090 --> 00:07:57,300
You could argue with a doctor
about pores and corrupted air.

143
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:00,820
It was much harder to defend a place
once it had become associated with the

144
00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:05,090
transmission of a disease that people
understood to have a specific route of spread.

145
00:08:05,110 --> 00:08:08,940
By the early 1500s, the shift was
visible even in casual writing.

146
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:14,620
The scholar Erasmus, in a dialogue published
in 1523, described public baths in

147
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:19,060
Brabant as far less popular than they
had been roughly 25 years earlier.

148
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:20,370
He wasn't writing a census.

149
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:23,870
He was writing literature, a
conversation between characters.

150
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:27,600
But even as a literary observation, it
tells us something about the changing

151
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:32,300
reputation of the bathhouse. Through the
16th century, public bathhouses declined

152
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:34,540
across large parts of Western Europe.

153
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:36,600
Not everywhere. Not at the same time.

154
00:08:36,620 --> 00:08:41,730
And not for one single reason. Plague-era
caution, the association with syphilis,

155
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:46,840
moral regulation, and changing ideas about
cleanliness all played different roles

156
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:50,920
depending on where you were. There's no
single year when you can say: this is when

157
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:54,110
Europe stopped bathing. Because
Europe didn't stop bathing.

158
00:08:54,320 --> 00:08:55,650
Public bathhouses declined.

159
00:08:55,880 --> 00:09:00,030
Bathing itself didn't. People
washed at home, in basins and tubs.

160
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:01,470
Medicinal baths continued.

161
00:09:01,660 --> 00:09:03,940
Spas continued. Sweat bathing continued.

162
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:05,810
The institution changed.

163
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:10,360
The practice didn't disappear. What happened
wasn't a collapse from clean to dirty.

164
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:15,300
It was a redistribution of cleanliness —
moving, gradually and unevenly, out of the

165
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:20,070
shared public bathhouse and into smaller,
more private, more controlled spaces.

166
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:21,730
So go back to where we started.

167
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:26,470
A hot bath was medicine. A hot bath was
also, to the same medical tradition, a

168
00:09:26,490 --> 00:09:30,320
potential danger. That wasn't a contradiction
to the people living inside that

169
00:09:30,340 --> 00:09:34,140
framework. It was the same idea
— heat and moisture changing the body —

170
00:09:34,160 --> 00:09:36,310
applied to two different situations.

171
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:39,380
In an ordinary year, opening
the body could help it heal.

172
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:43,560
In a year when the air itself was believed
to carry disease, opening the body could

173
00:09:43,580 --> 00:09:44,950
help disease get in.

174
00:09:44,980 --> 00:09:48,760
Nobody in 1348 woke up and
decided to hate water.

175
00:09:48,780 --> 00:09:53,040
A king asked his physicians a serious
question, and they gave him the most logical

176
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:54,830
answer their medicine could produce.

177
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:56,580
They were wrong about the mechanism.

178
00:09:56,600 --> 00:09:58,360
But some of what they observed was real.

179
00:09:58,380 --> 00:10:01,810
Crowded rooms during an
epidemic could be dangerous.

180
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:04,170
Contact with infected people could matter.

181
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:07,620
And some physicians were already beginning
to argue that disease could spread

182
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:09,590
directly from person to person.

183
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:13,480
Medieval Europe never made the simple
journey from clean to dirty that we like to

184
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:17,290
imagine. What actually happened was
more complicated, and more human.

185
00:10:17,310 --> 00:10:22,120
An institution built around washing,
healing, and social life collided with new

186
00:10:22,140 --> 00:10:27,660
diseases, new fears, new regulations,
and slowly changing ideas about what

187
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:31,380
cleanliness should look like. The bath never
stopped working the way they thought it

188
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:34,720
worked. The world around it just
kept changing what that meant.

189
00:10:34,860 --> 00:10:38,390
And if you made it this far, the sources
for this story are in the description.

190
00:10:38,410 --> 00:10:42,240
If you enjoyed the video, hit that like
button and subscribe, and I will see you in

191
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:43,260
the next one.
